rm9Assessment.text:
The author's survivor RM9 (drop `if (recorded) return append();`) is **not an equivalent mutant**. I wrote a probe row (untracked, removed after use; template below) that stubs the store-level `getCommandReceipt` to miss once, which is exactly what a racing writer between the async store-level read and the IMMEDIATE transaction produces. On GREEN bytes the probe passes (replay returns `committed`, no guard, no hook, no notification, no second saved-record rewrite). Under RM9 the probe fails with `['guard','notify']` - the mutant re-takes M, re-runs the extension and re-fires the commit notification on a replay; under RV3 it fails with `['not-committed']`. So one row closes the class. Reachability in production is confined to a concurrent writer on the same campaign journal outside the host's single-writer lock, which today does not exist; severity Low. Template (`rm9-probe.cjs` sha256 d550d94b...6eb8 ran it):

```ts
jest.spyOn(store, 'getCommandReceipt').mockResolvedValueOnce(null);
const again = await store.appendControlledCommandBatch!('campaign-journal', input, {
  guard: () => { log.push('guard'); return null; },
  onCommitted: () => log.push('notify'),
  onNotCommitted: () => log.push('not-committed'),
});
expect(again.kind).toBe('committed'); expect(log).toEqual([]); expect(rewrites).toBe(1);
```
